n's HTTPS proxy follow the new environment's allowed hosts, and credentials saved on the previous environment stop being used. What does not: connections that are already open keep their earlier reach, tools that bypass the proxy keep this machine's earlier network rules until the machine is replaced, the environment's variables are not set on this machine until its next restart, and the setup script does not run on this machine. Where this session cannot be moved this way, the environment is still created and the result says that the session stayed where it is, and why, unless the call carries `leave_statement`: then it is refused and nothing is created. \"none\": the environment is created and this session stays where it is. There is no default: a call without `switch_session` is refused.", "enum": ["network_only", "none"], "type": "string"}}, "required": ["name", "scope", "reason", "switch_session"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__create_environment"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "Create a new Claude Code Remote session. Returns the new session's ID and status. If environment_id is omitted, the new session inherits the calling session's environment. Combine with send_message for fan-out orchestration: spawn a sibling and send it a task. Where enabled, this session receives a <child-session-event> turn if the new session's turn fails or its worker restarts and drops background tasks; a session that finishes cleanly may not report back, so check on it with get_session (status_bucket reads 'failed' for a turn that errored, where status alone reads 'idle' either way) and list_events.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__create_session", "schema": {"description": "Create a new Claude Code Remote session. Returns the new session's ID and status. If environment_id is omitted, the new session inherits the calling session's environment. Combine with send_message for fan-out orchestration: spawn a sibling and send it a task. Where 